{
    "title": "Air Plan Approval; California; South Coast Air Quality Management District",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 11, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-11/pdf/2025-22584.pdf",
    "summary": "The latest Federal Register includes three updates: a temporary safety zone for New Year\u2019s Eve fireworks on the Maumee River in Ohio, and two Environmental Protection Agency approvals that give California\u2019s South Coast and Arizona\u2019s West Pinal County the green light to keep moving forward on clean air goals.\n\nFor those in and around Sandusky, the Coast Guard will enforce a safety zone on the Maumee River during the New Year\u2019s Eve celebration, including around midnight. Boats and people won\u2019t be allowed in the area without permission to keep everyone safe during the fireworks. This helps spectators, event organizers, and first responders by reducing risk on the water, though boaters and businesses that depend on river access may face short-term disruptions during the event window.\n\nIn Southern California, the EPA approved a South Coast Air Quality Management District rule that sets up a fee program required when an area misses federal smog standards. The rule takes effect January 12, 2026. The aim is to keep pressure on reducing ozone-forming pollution so the region continues improving air quality. Residents, especially those with asthma or other respiratory issues, stand to benefit from cleaner air and clearer accountability. Some businesses may see higher costs tied to the fee program, but they also get regulatory certainty and a level playing field, since the rule aligns local policy with federal expectations and doesn\u2019t add new federal requirements beyond state law.\n\nIn Arizona, the EPA fully approved the state\u2019s approach to permits for large industrial sources in West Pinal County to address dust pollution tied to an older federal standard. This also becomes effective January 12, 2026. Communities in the area could see health and visibility benefits as new or expanding facilities meet stronger permit conditions. Companies planning projects may face added steps and potential costs to meet the rules, but they gain predictability about what\u2019s required to build and operate.\n\nOverall, these actions emphasize safety for a local celebration and steady, locally led progress on air quality. The benefits are public safety and cleaner air; the trade-offs are limited access on the river for a few hours and potential compliance costs for certain industries.",
